# Spacecraft
<!--{ "parent": "world", "order": 6 }-->

*Spacecraft are just [vehicles](/vehicles/) — but the **drive** you give them is one of the biggest decisions a science-fiction setting makes, so they get their own page and a set of sample fleets. Pick a setting's row of the table below and you've picked how its universe *feels*.*

The drive decides everything downstream — how long a journey takes, whether space combat is dogfighting or gunnery, whether the campaign map is a cluster of stars or a single solar system:

| Setting | Sublight | FTL | Feels like |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Space Opera** | flies like an aeroplane | instant hyperdrive jump | swashbuckling; space is an ocean |
| **Warp** | fractional-*c* impulse | superluminal *cruise* (or fixed gates) | exploration; travel time is a dial |
| **Newtonian** | constant-thrust fusion torch | none, or a fixed mass-shunt / magic gate | hard SF; vectors and reaction mass (ForeSight's own model — and *The Expanse*'s) |
| **Culture** | trivially near-*c* | near-instant, grid-powered | post-scarcity; the *ship* is the biggest character |

**Newtonian is ForeSight's native setting**: you burn toward your destination, flip at the midpoint, and burn to stop — there is no free lunch and no FTL unless a **mass shunt** (or, in a fantasy-SF game, a **magic gate**) links fixed points. The other three are here so you can lift a familiar feel off the shelf. Big hulls use the [Damage](/damage/) Scale; a capital ship is a setpiece, not a stat line you dogfight.

